  "prediction": "The report outlines the various budget documents and resources available to the public regarding the U.S. federal budget. The budget year begins with the President's submission of a proposed budget to Congress, which includes an annual budget report, a detailed budget justification, and supporting documents such as historical tables and economic reports. These documents are made available online by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Government Publishing Office (GPO).\n\nThe President's budget proposal is followed by congressional hearings and the appropriations process, during which individual committees and subcommittees consider and modify the budget requests. The progress of appropriations bills is tracked by the Congressional Research Service (CRS) and can be accessed on their website.\n\nAdditional resources include the Economic Report of the President, which provides an analysis of the nation's economic conditions, and the Congressional Budget Office (CBO) reports, which provide independent analyses of the budget and economic projections. The CBO also maintains a public version of its budget data and projections online.\n\nPrinted copies of budget documents can be purchased from the GPO, and the CRS provides web pages covering the budget and appropriations process for members of Congress and the public. Overall, the report provides a comprehensive overview of the various budget documents and resources available to the public throughout the federal budget process.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the various budget documents and resources available to the public regarding the U.S. federal budget. The budget year begins with the President's submission of a proposed budget to Congress, which includes an annual budget report, a detailed budget justification, and supporting documents such as historical tables and economic reports. These documents are made available online by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Government Publishing Office (GPO).\n\nThe President's budget proposal is followed by congressional hearings and the appropriations process, during which individual committees and subcommittees consider and modify the budget requests. The progress of appropriations bills is tracked by the Congressional Research Service (CRS) and can be accessed on their website.\n\nAdditional resources include the Economic Report of the President, which provides an analysis of the nation's economic conditions, and the Congressional Budget Office (CBO) reports, which provide independent analyses of the budget and economic projections. The CBO also maintains a public version of its budget data and projections online.\n\nPrinted copies of budget documents can be purchased from the GPO, and the CRS provides web pages covering the budget and appropriations process for members of Congress and the public. Overall, the report provides a comprehensive overview of the various budget documents and resources available to the public throughout the federal budget process.",
  "score": 29.203539323361273,
  "answers": [
    "Every year the President submits a series of volumes to Congress containing the President's proposed budget for the coming fiscal year. The President's submission is required on or after the first Monday in January, but no later than the first Monday in February (31 U.S.C. \u00a71105(a)). This year the President released the budget submission, in two installments, on March 11, and March 18, 2019. This report provides brief descriptions of the FY2020 budget volumes and related documents, together with internet addresses, Government Publishing Office (GPO) stock numbers, and prices for obtaining print copies of these publications. It also explains how to find the locations of government depository libraries, which can provide both printed copies for reference use and internet access to the online versions. This report will be updated as events warrant. Please note that neither the Congressional Research Service (CRS) nor the Library of Congress (LOC) distributes print copies of the budget documents."
  ],
